  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nQuiet weather as of 1pm today with temperatures in the upper 70s\nto low 80s. A fairly broad cu field has developed across much of\neastern Nebraska, indicative of relatively steep surface lapse\nrates capped by the strong inversion around 5000ft. Looking at\nthe broader upper-level pattern we have broad ridging over the\nRockies with a deep trough over the Mississippi River Valley.\nThis puts us in our fairly active northwesterly flow regime. \n\nFor this evening we're watching a cold front approach from the\nwestern Dakotas that will move into northeast Nebraska this\nevening. Ahead of this front we'll see moisture pooling and\nsteepening lapse rates generating around 2500 J/kg of CAPE just\nahead of the frontal boundary. This combined with increasing\n0-6km bulk shear could lead to a few organized storms starting\nto develop around 4-5 PM. Limiting factor will be moisture as\nthe front stalls just to our north and west, weakening through\nthe evening. With this we'll have a window from around 4pm to\n10pm of a conditional threat for strong to severe storms over\nnortheast Nebraska. If we can get anything to develop, it has a\ngood chance of becoming severe with primary threats being\ndamaging winds to 75 mph and large hail up to 2 inches in \ndiameter. Anything that develops should dissipate by 10pm as\ntemperatures aloft warm and the environment becomes more \nstable.\n\nOvernight we could see some patchy shallow fog develop, but \nsignificant dry air in the lower levels should significantly\nlimit extent, more likely in low-lying areas particularly near\nwater. We'll be watching for more storms developing over our \nwestern counties starting around 6-7 AM as the next shortwave \ninteracts with a theta-e gradient stretching from Central NE \nsoutheast toward Gage County. As this shortwave clears around \nnoon we should see scattered storms wane and mostly clear to the\nsoutheast; however, a few hi-res models including the HRRR keep\na few scattered showers and storms through the afternoon. CAPE \nonly increases into the afternoon with daytime heating, but we \nwill be lacking much of a trigger. Any storms that develop could\nbecome strong to severe through the afternoon with 50 to 60 kt \nof 0-6km bulk shear with hazards mainly being damaging winds and\nlarge hail again. Low-level shear will be much weaker, but \ncan't completely rule out a tornado threat. \n\nA third shortwave will trigger a better chance for strong to\nsevere storms Friday evening through around midnight. Storms\ninitiate as discrete storms just west of Boone County, likely \norganizing into a cluster of storms and a bowing line riding \nthis same theta-e gradient into southeast Nebraska. \n\nNorthwest flow will continue to bring shortwaves through leading\nto periodic chances for showers and storms through the weekend\ninto Monday. The ridge to our west builds eastward into next\nweek, weakening and deamplifying as it does. A strong jet\nstreak passing over the top of the ridge will keep this unstable\nflow regime into next week likely with more periodic chances for\nshowers and storms. Temperatures stay on the milder side for\nAugust with highs generally in the low-to-mid 80s and overnight\nlows in the low-to-mid 60s.",
